1024
00:46:07.460 --> 00:46:12.840
Listen, if you ever have an emergency, or you need to take a depressed boyfriend out to lunch

1025
00:46:12.840 --> 00:46:14.460
or something, let me know.

1026
00:46:14.680 --> 00:46:15.960
I'll cover the phones for you.

1027
00:46:16.160 --> 00:46:16.600
Shh.

1028
00:46:16.880 --> 00:46:17.140
Wow.

1029
00:46:17.560 --> 00:46:17.740
Yeah.

1030
00:46:17.940 --> 00:46:19.160
That is so nice of you.

1031
00:46:19.520 --> 00:46:21.520
But do you know how to use the phone system?

1032
00:46:21.800 --> 00:46:22.560
Are you kidding me?

1033
00:46:22.920 --> 00:46:23.120
No.

1034
00:46:23.360 --> 00:46:24.020
Check me out.

1035
00:46:26.470 --> 00:46:26.970
Get in here.

1036
00:46:30.540 --> 00:46:31.560
Dunder Mifflin, this is Pete.

1037
00:46:31.920 --> 00:46:32.420
Very good.

1038
00:46:32.420 --> 00:46:35.120
But what if you have to take a phone message?

1039
00:46:35.600 --> 00:46:36.180
Play it on me.

1040
00:46:36.580 --> 00:46:37.360
Take a pen.

1041
00:46:37.480 --> 00:46:37.660
Mm-hmm.

1042
00:46:37.960 --> 00:46:38.980
Take a piece of paper.

1043
00:46:39.820 --> 00:46:40.860
Write down the message.

1044
00:46:41.060 --> 00:46:41.400
Oh, I don't know.

1045
00:46:41.520 --> 00:46:42.260
I think there's...

1046
00:46:42.960 --> 00:46:44.060
I think there's someone on the phone.

1047
00:46:44.940 --> 00:46:46.120
Dunder Mifflin, this is Erin.

1048
00:46:49.450 --> 00:46:52.070
It was really sweet when he says, oh, yeah, I got this.

1049
00:46:52.150 --> 00:46:56.730
He picks up the wrong phone, is writing on the wrong thing, and then she is showing him

1050
00:46:56.730 --> 00:46:57.530
how to write a note.

1051
00:46:57.690 --> 00:47:00.210
But then there's an actual person calling and on the phone.

1052
00:47:00.250 --> 00:47:00.390
Yeah, yeah.

1053
00:47:00.690 --> 00:47:01.910
But it's really cute.

1054
00:47:02.070 --> 00:47:03.790
But he's there for her.

1055
00:47:03.790 --> 00:47:06.910
He's like, if you need to go cheer up Andy, I'll cover you.

1056
00:47:07.490 --> 00:47:09.670
Well, unfortunately, Andy's not interested.

1057
00:47:10.190 --> 00:47:10.690
Yeah.

1058
00:47:11.190 --> 00:47:13.750
But then Erin has an idea he is interested in.

1059
00:47:14.170 --> 00:47:19.150
Why don't we go sail that boat one time before you give it up?

1060
00:47:19.650 --> 00:47:21.950
I don't care that it's in Stamford, Connecticut.

1061
00:47:21.950 --> 00:47:23.350
We got to leave right now.

1062
00:47:23.470 --> 00:47:24.630
But let's do it.

1063
00:47:25.330 --> 00:47:26.450
Andy is so excited.

1064
00:47:27.170 --> 00:47:28.790
I think it's a sweet idea.

1065
00:47:29.450 --> 00:47:30.430
I do, too.

1066
00:47:30.670 --> 00:47:31.930
To go say goodbye to the boat.

1067
00:47:32.290 --> 00:47:32.790
Yeah.

1068
00:47:32.790 --> 00:47:32.870
Yeah.

1069
00:47:33.050 --> 00:47:34.350
That's an important ritual.

1070
00:47:34.790 --> 00:47:35.830
I think so.

1071
00:47:36.310 --> 00:47:38.850
Well, the next scene might be the final straw for Kevin.

1072
00:47:39.710 --> 00:47:44.690
Angela gets off the phone with the senator and says, oh, the senator's exhausted from

1073
00:47:44.690 --> 00:47:45.390
the campaign.

1074
00:47:45.990 --> 00:47:51.190
He's up against a man who's so dirty, and the senator keeps pushing back as hard as he

1075
00:47:51.190 --> 00:47:51.610
can.

1076
00:47:52.490 --> 00:47:53.990
Kevin's like, you have to stop.

1077
00:47:54.230 --> 00:47:57.930
And then Angela says, last night he was so tired he just wanted a little Mexican brought

1078
00:47:57.930 --> 00:47:58.210
in.

1079
00:47:58.210 --> 00:48:00.830
Kevin is hysterical.

1080
00:48:01.110 --> 00:48:02.290
It's too much for him.

1081
00:48:02.510 --> 00:48:03.030
He leaves.

1082
00:48:03.790 --> 00:48:06.970
I thought this scene was very funny, but I was a little bit distracted.

1083
00:48:07.310 --> 00:48:09.530
Angela, we have a new local mug report.

1084
00:48:10.130 --> 00:48:10.590
Oh.

1085
00:48:11.030 --> 00:48:14.430
Kevin has a mug on his desk from Acker Drill Company.

1086
00:48:14.830 --> 00:48:20.190
It was founded in 1916, and they're one of North America's largest manufacturers of drilling

1087
00:48:20.190 --> 00:48:23.510
equipment worldwide, new local mug report.

1088
00:48:23.930 --> 00:48:24.990
There you go.

1089
00:48:25.450 --> 00:48:28.290
I had my eyes open for the mugs on this episode.

1090
00:48:28.630 --> 00:48:30.710
Clearly, they were speaking to you.

1091
00:48:30.950 --> 00:48:31.250
Yep.

1092
00:48:31.250 --> 00:48:36.910
Well, right after Kevin rushes off, Oscar has a talking head where he says, I'm in big

1093
00:48:36.910 --> 00:48:37.230
trouble.

1094
00:48:37.390 --> 00:48:41.110
And then Kevin has a talking head where he says, Oscar's in big trouble.

1095
00:48:42.570 --> 00:48:46.770
And Oscar's going to do something very unethical.

1096
00:48:47.070 --> 00:48:48.410
He's got this folder.

1097
00:48:48.770 --> 00:48:50.730
A sabotage folder.

1098
00:48:50.950 --> 00:48:51.450
Yes.

1099
00:48:51.630 --> 00:48:56.250
It has, I guess, information about Kevin not doing his job well.

1100
00:48:56.250 --> 00:49:01.350
He's going to take it back to Toby, and we had a fan question from Jasmine B.

1101
00:49:01.730 --> 00:49:08.370
In Southern Manitoba, Jasmine said, why does Toby start wearing glasses all of a sudden?

1102
00:49:08.550 --> 00:49:12.530
He continues to wear glasses for a time after this episode.

1103
00:49:12.850 --> 00:49:17.190
Was this intentional, or did Paul just start wearing glasses and nobody cared?

1104
00:49:17.870 --> 00:49:19.370
Jasmine, correct.

1105
00:49:19.930 --> 00:49:24.350
Paul needed to start wearing glasses, so we just put him on Toby.

1106
00:49:24.610 --> 00:49:25.690
We never discuss it.

1107
00:49:26.250 --> 00:49:26.830
It just happened.

1108
00:49:27.250 --> 00:49:32.570
Paul had always had glasses, and I think he just decided to start wearing them.

1109
00:49:33.110 --> 00:49:33.630
Yeah.

1110
00:49:33.850 --> 00:49:35.230
I think it kind of tracks, though.

1111
00:49:35.490 --> 00:49:41.650
I could see Toby as a character suddenly coming into the office with glasses and not a single

1112
00:49:41.650 --> 00:49:42.690
person mentioning it.

1113
00:49:43.050 --> 00:49:43.290
Yeah.

1114
00:49:43.810 --> 00:49:48.390
So I think it's very realistic that we never refer to these sudden glasses.

1115
00:49:48.390 --> 00:49:56.330
Well, as Toby analyzes this Kevin folder, he's like, wow, Kevin's gambling problem has resurfaced.

1116
00:49:56.550 --> 00:49:57.190
You know what?

1117
00:49:57.330 --> 00:50:01.130
I'm going to have to send Kevin home until I can investigate further.

1118
00:50:01.450 --> 00:50:03.490
So Toby summons Kevin.

1119
00:50:04.230 --> 00:50:04.590
Mm-hmm.

1120
00:50:05.030 --> 00:50:06.170
Kevin's actually excited.

1121
00:50:06.170 --> 00:50:07.610
He thinks he's getting a promotion.

1122
00:50:07.610 --> 00:50:10.150
I know, I know.

1123
00:50:10.330 --> 00:50:11.790
Oscar feels bad.

1124
00:50:12.170 --> 00:50:12.510
Yeah.

1125
00:50:12.770 --> 00:50:16.050
We had a fan catch from Ashley S.

1126
00:50:16.230 --> 00:50:21.470
in California who said, at 11 minutes and 14 seconds, Oscar

1127
00:50:21.470 --> 00:50:27.770
has a yellow sunburst sticker on his side of the partition between Kevin and Angela's desks.

1128
00:50:27.810 --> 00:50:30.730
It's right above a framed portrait of a dog.

1129
00:50:30.730 --> 00:50:34.090
Is that sticker from Tobin James Cellars?

1130
00:50:34.350 --> 00:50:39.830
It is a winery in Paso Robles, California, and it looks identical to their logo.

1131
00:50:39.830 --> 00:50:43.990
I live in Paso Robles and was so excited to see it.

1132
00:50:44.270 --> 00:50:48.070
Well, Ashley, I did a little side-by-side.

1133
00:50:48.630 --> 00:50:50.730
Oh, I love a side-by-side.

1134
00:50:50.970 --> 00:50:51.670
I know.

1135
00:50:51.810 --> 00:50:53.250
I love a side-by-side as well.

1136
00:50:53.790 --> 00:50:56.550
Angela, I think Ashley is correct.

1137
00:50:56.550 --> 00:50:57.530
What do you think?

1138
00:50:57.970 --> 00:50:58.770
Oh, yeah.

1139
00:50:59.330 --> 00:50:59.750
Right?

1140
00:51:00.290 --> 00:51:03.230
My theory is that Oscar Nunez put that there.

1141
00:51:03.470 --> 00:51:04.270
Me too.

1142
00:51:04.550 --> 00:51:05.290
Me too.

1143
00:51:05.610 --> 00:51:07.570
Because that is a framed photo of his dog.

1144
00:51:07.910 --> 00:51:08.290
Mm-hmm.

1145
00:51:08.430 --> 00:51:10.170
And maybe he went wine tasting.

1146
00:51:10.170 --> 00:51:12.410
He just put the sticker there when he got to work.

1147
00:51:13.050 --> 00:51:14.290
Great catch, Ashley.

1148
00:51:14.810 --> 00:51:20.770
When Toby is speaking to Kevin, he is quickly interrupted by Oscar, who gives Kevin some money

1149
00:51:20.770 --> 00:51:26.130
to go get something out of the vending machine, and he fesses up to Toby that it's not true.

1150
00:51:26.310 --> 00:51:27.370
It's just not true.

1151
00:51:27.370 --> 00:51:30.550
He falsified the numbers, and he did it.

1152
00:51:30.690 --> 00:51:33.070
He set him up because he was annoyed.

1153
00:51:33.070 --> 00:51:34.070
They had an argument.

1154
00:51:34.330 --> 00:51:35.470
He was trying to get back at him.

1155
00:51:35.590 --> 00:51:36.090
You know what?

1156
00:51:36.110 --> 00:51:41.050
It doesn't even matter what Oscar's saying to Toby about Kevin, because all Toby's doing

1157
00:51:41.050 --> 00:51:44.530
is thinking back to that case with the Scranton Strangler.

1158
00:51:44.530 --> 00:51:47.950
He's like, oh, no, I suspected this all along.

1159
00:51:48.230 --> 00:51:53.190
A few years ago when I was on the Scranton Strangler jury, I thought he had been set up.

1160
00:51:53.390 --> 00:51:54.710
I felt pressured to convict.

1161
00:51:54.970 --> 00:51:56.770
I put away an innocent man.

1162
00:51:57.110 --> 00:51:59.650
He's not even really listening to what Oscar is saying.

1163
00:51:59.650 --> 00:52:02.230
No, he heard the word set up, and it triggered.

1164
00:52:02.450 --> 00:52:09.190
It just triggered this fear that the Scranton Strangler was actually set up and that he convicted

1165
00:52:09.190 --> 00:52:10.030
the wrong person.

1166
00:52:10.370 --> 00:52:12.510
And here, he almost did it again, right?

1167
00:52:12.710 --> 00:52:12.870
Yeah.

1168
00:52:12.870 --> 00:52:16.750
He almost convicted Kevin of a false crime.

1169
00:52:17.170 --> 00:52:17.470
Yeah.

1170
00:52:17.650 --> 00:52:19.810
He can't let this keep happening in his life.

1171
00:52:20.050 --> 00:52:24.610
Listen, lady, if you thought I was only going to track two mugs in this episode, I've got news

1172
00:52:24.610 --> 00:52:25.050
for you.

1173
00:52:25.310 --> 00:52:26.190
Another mug?

1174
00:52:26.190 --> 00:52:29.770
Yes, this time, it is with the help of Shelby P.

1175
00:52:29.990 --> 00:52:31.650
from Louisville, Kentucky, who said,

1176
00:52:31.830 --> 00:52:37.370
when Oscar comes back to Toby's desk, there is a yellow NBC mug on Toby's desk.

1177
00:52:37.490 --> 00:52:39.090
It's very clearly in the shot.

1178
00:52:39.390 --> 00:52:45.290
Did Paul leave this there by mistake, or was this an on-purpose nod to NBC?

1179
00:52:46.230 --> 00:52:54.910
Well, Shelby, it was an NBC mug, but it was for the local Scranton station of NBC, WBRE.

1180
00:52:54.910 --> 00:52:57.350
I love a local station shout-out.

1181
00:52:57.610 --> 00:52:57.950
Mm-hmm.

1182
00:52:58.490 --> 00:53:03.530
And we also got this tidbit, Ange, from Jessica Jay in St. Paul, Minnesota, who said,

1183
00:53:03.710 --> 00:53:08.690
I just want to say that I'm mad at Oscar for being willing to get Kevin fired in order to

1184
00:53:08.690 --> 00:53:10.530
keep his own betrayal a secret.

1185
00:53:11.270 --> 00:53:15.290
And when he came clean to Toby, he didn't even get in trouble for it.

1186
00:53:16.250 --> 00:53:18.330
Jessica, I kind of thought the same thing.

1187
00:53:18.970 --> 00:53:21.830
But Toby's spinning out about the Scranton Strangler.

1188
00:53:21.830 --> 00:53:22.510
Exactly.

1189
00:53:22.750 --> 00:53:24.910
Toby's in his head about his own stuff.

1190
00:53:25.110 --> 00:53:27.530
And so Oscar skates free on this one.

1191
00:53:28.210 --> 00:53:29.430
Here's my question.

1192
00:53:29.430 --> 00:53:32.410
And this is, I'm going to open up a convo about it.

1193
00:53:32.630 --> 00:53:33.550
A conflab.

1194
00:53:34.310 --> 00:53:34.830
Conflab?

1195
00:53:34.950 --> 00:53:35.810
What's a conflab?

1196
00:53:35.990 --> 00:53:40.110
It's like a conversation where you discuss something.

1197
00:53:40.310 --> 00:53:42.070
It's from that TV show Glow Up.

1198
00:53:42.430 --> 00:53:45.430
It's the makeup challenge show from England.

1199
00:53:45.430 --> 00:53:46.670
They have a conflab.

1200
00:53:46.670 --> 00:53:49.130
Oh, a flabby convo.

1201
00:53:49.330 --> 00:53:49.750
Let's go.

1202
00:53:49.950 --> 00:53:50.150
Yeah.

1203
00:53:50.430 --> 00:53:50.650
Okay.

1204
00:53:50.830 --> 00:53:52.350
So here's my conflab.

1205
00:53:52.690 --> 00:53:56.950
Do you think that Oscar fabricated the numbers in that folder?

1206
00:53:57.190 --> 00:53:59.230
Or do you think that they were real?

1207
00:53:59.430 --> 00:54:04.810
Because I think he really has been keeping a folder on Kevin,

1208
00:54:04.810 --> 00:54:15.190
maybe in case he ever needed to defend himself or the company against some sort of, I don't know.

1209
00:54:15.370 --> 00:54:16.290
You know what I mean?

1210
00:54:16.670 --> 00:54:17.130
Oh.

1211
00:54:17.290 --> 00:54:20.750
And he handed, I don't think he'd like sat down and made this up.

1212
00:54:20.870 --> 00:54:24.110
I think this is a secret file folder that Oscar has been keeping.

1213
00:54:24.110 --> 00:54:25.710
One hundred percent.

1214
00:54:26.210 --> 00:54:27.530
Oscar is a great accountant.

1215
00:54:27.970 --> 00:54:30.710
He's seen all the discrepancies on Kevin's side

1216
00:54:30.710 --> 00:54:36.650
of the desk, and he's been keeping tabs to cover his own ass and has that folder ready to play it

1217
00:54:36.650 --> 00:54:37.470
when he needs it.

1218
00:54:37.750 --> 00:54:40.530
But he never thought he would play it for personal reasons.

1219
00:54:40.750 --> 00:54:41.370
That's right.

1220
00:54:41.950 --> 00:54:42.630
Okay, good.

1221
00:54:42.750 --> 00:54:43.170
We agree.

1222
00:54:43.770 --> 00:54:44.570
Conflab over.

1223
00:54:45.210 --> 00:54:45.930
Conflab done.

1224
00:54:46.390 --> 00:54:49.870
I think we should go back and take a peek into Dwight's interview.

1225
00:54:50.710 --> 00:54:51.590
He's nude.

1226
00:54:52.110 --> 00:54:54.010
He's in the break room.

1227
00:54:54.410 --> 00:54:56.750
He has on underwear.

1228
00:54:57.670 --> 00:55:01.070
I'm sorry, he has on his green underwear, which is his favorite.

1229
00:55:01.310 --> 00:55:02.470
His green tighty-whities.

1230
00:55:02.890 --> 00:55:03.410
Yes.

1231
00:55:03.930 --> 00:55:07.990
Yeah, Dwight is really upset because Iris Black says, you know what?

1232
00:55:08.090 --> 00:55:10.490
We have found out that this paper is toxic.

1233
00:55:10.610 --> 00:55:11.930
Dunder Mifflin paper is toxic.

1234
00:55:12.190 --> 00:55:14.430
And Dwight's like, no, this is slanderous.

1235
00:55:14.510 --> 00:55:16.730
And he says, I need a credible source.

1236
00:55:16.730 --> 00:55:18.250
And guess what?

1237
00:55:18.810 --> 00:55:23.550
Iris just happens to have the New York foreman, Sandra Mick, on the line.

1238
00:55:23.990 --> 00:55:26.450
Jenna, this character is hilarious.

1239
00:55:26.870 --> 00:55:27.210
Yeah.

1240
00:55:27.490 --> 00:55:29.190
How did you come up with her voice?

1241
00:55:29.190 --> 00:55:30.610
It's so raspy.

1242
00:55:30.610 --> 00:55:32.630
You sound like you've been chain-smoking

1243
00:55:32.630 --> 00:55:33.510
for years.

1244
00:55:34.010 --> 00:55:35.030
This was hilarious.

1245
00:55:35.430 --> 00:55:37.310
The minute you opened your mouth, I forgot.

1246
00:55:37.370 --> 00:55:39.150
I forgot about Sandra Mick.

1247
00:55:39.290 --> 00:55:40.310
I hadn't seen this

1248
00:55:40.310 --> 00:55:41.350
episode since it aired.

1249
00:55:41.510 --> 00:55:45.570
And when Nellie throws this to you, and then you open your mouth and

1250
00:55:45.570 --> 00:55:47.030
speak, I was like, what?

1251
00:55:47.790 --> 00:55:52.310
Lady, I was as surprised as you were by the voice that came out of my mouth.

1252
00:55:52.530 --> 00:55:53.790
Everybody in the room

1253
00:55:53.790 --> 00:55:55.070
was shocked.

1254
00:55:55.410 --> 00:55:56.850
You look surprised.

1255
00:55:57.730 --> 00:56:00.530
You kind of do this thing with your hand where you go like, oh.

1256
00:56:00.990 --> 00:56:06.630
Yes, I got some phlegm stuck in my throat when I went to do this, and it created this weird,

1257
00:56:06.630 --> 00:56:08.030
raspy voice.

1258
00:56:08.090 --> 00:56:11.550
The script did not specify any accent.

1259
00:56:11.550 --> 00:56:16.670
It just said, Pam speaks, voice passably disguised.

1260
00:56:16.990 --> 00:56:18.750
It was very disguised.

1261
00:56:18.910 --> 00:56:21.570
I would never know that was you or Pam.

1262
00:56:22.050 --> 00:56:24.190
The paper is not toxic.

1263
00:56:24.850 --> 00:56:28.630
Unless it's exposed to oxygen, then it's very toxic.

1264
00:56:28.990 --> 00:56:31.910
Which, at the same time, I thought was very quick for Pam.

1265
00:56:32.130 --> 00:56:33.610
I was like, oh, that's a good point.

1266
00:56:33.610 --> 00:56:34.930
Dwight would believe that.

1267
00:56:35.490 --> 00:56:40.010
Jim is going to barge in and tell Dwight that their stock prices are

1268
00:56:40.010 --> 00:56:40.730
plummeting.

1269
00:56:42.110 --> 00:56:43.530
And Jim says, you know what?

1270
00:56:43.590 --> 00:56:45.990
Do we need to get someone who understands the media?

1271
00:56:46.190 --> 00:56:47.890
And Dwight says, get out.

1272
00:56:48.050 --> 00:56:48.590
Get out of the room.

1273
00:56:48.770 --> 00:56:50.910
Their little tussle in the break room cracked me

1274
00:56:50.910 --> 00:56:51.210
up.

1275
00:56:51.370 --> 00:56:52.390
See, this is the thing.

1276
00:56:52.570 --> 00:56:54.630
These are the early days of the office for me.

